  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2022 Leuven University Press book is positioned as a practical guide for teaching European citizenship, with the strongest evidence of impact coming from sustained use rather than citation. It has 1,994 recorded downloads, is openly accessible in hybrid form, and is aligned with the SDG theme of Peace, Justice and strong institutions. The record also shows institutional and EU-related funding support, but only a very small public discussion trail and no recorded citations yet.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Europees burgerschap in de klas addresses a timely educational and civic question: how to help young people understand the European Union\u2019s influence on everyday life and become informed, critical citizens. The book is framed as a practical handbook for teachers in secondary and higher education, and its subject profile spans humanities, political science, philosophy, theology, and art, reflecting its interdisciplinary approach to civic learning.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest evidence of reach is usage. The book has been downloaded 1,994 times through OAPEN, suggesting that readers are accessing it as a working resource rather than only encountering it in citation networks. Its open availability in a hybrid model likely supports this pattern, making the text easier to consult in teaching and professional contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader signal set points to policy and public-interest relevance. Its alignment with Peace, Justice and strong institutions fits the book\u2019s focus on democratic participation, responsibility, and European citizenship education. Funding from KU Leuven, the College of Europe, the Fonds Lenaerts-Grimonprez, and the European Union also indicates institutional backing for a topic with educational and civic significance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record is still quiet, with no citations recorded in OpenAlex at the time of this digest. That does not diminish the book\u2019s apparent utility; rather, it suggests that the available evidence today is concentrated in access, adoption potential, and mission-oriented relevance. The public-discussion trace is limited, so the main story is one of practical uptake and accessible dissemination, not yet of measured scholarly accumulation.</p>",
